  "publication_text": "We have repeated 6 of the 14 flagged cases with both released Ohm SDKs. Each comparison has four pairs, with both SDKs running on the same machine. The paired 95% intervals showed that 2 cases were slower. The 95% intervals for 4 cases span zero.\n\n| Case | Ohm 1.99.0-34 | Ohm 1.100.0-1 | Mean change | 95% interval for change |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Ruff developer workflow \u00b7 edit-check-test-check | 21.59 s | 25.69 s | +4.10 s | -0.39 to +8.59 s |\n| Ruff editing replay \u00b7 Run final strict Clippy for both affected packages | 15.29 s | 17.22 s | +1.93 s | -0.26 to +4.11 s |\n| uv editing replay \u00b7 Run local-mock-index installation integration test | 38.04 s | 41.13 s | +3.09 s | +1.71 to +4.47 s |\n| Ruff historical replay \u00b7 Run strict Clippy for the changed core and semantic crates | 19.67 s | 20.25 s | +0.58 s | -1.05 to +2.20 s |\n| ty editing replay \u00b7 Run all semantic package tests | 48.41 s | 51.99 s | +3.58 s | -0.39 to +7.55 s |\n| ty editing replay \u00b7 Run Clippy for all semantic targets and features | 26.13 s | 30.45 s | +4.33 s | +2.13 to +6.52 s |\n\nPositive changes mean the new release took longer. The comparisons include the upstream Rust version change and Ohm changes. Both uv SDKs used the same import-level Clippy allowance. Both Ruff SDKs ran Clippy with `-D warnings`. The 1.99 command omits one allowance for the newer `clippy::needless_nonzero_get` lint. Eight Codex cases are still being compared. On the second host, two stock/current control pairs averaged 493.43 seconds with Rust 1.100 and 311.12 with Ohm. The source, lockfile and all 3,439 final TUI tests matched the original host.",
  "method": "Four order-balanced pairs per completed case. Two-sided 95% intervals use paired differences and Student t with three degrees of freedom."
}
